														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1417447">
									

<p>How, precisely, is this supposed to be a Euclid? I mean, there's nothing that <em>forces</em> someone to do the stupid thing and get into the car, and even that could still be considered safe (provided definite limits to the area and it being a passive effect). Euclid means that the SCP has an inherent element of unpredictability and/or uncertainty. Putting it another way, a safe SCP is something you can build a structure around, lock the structure up, and then safely forget it exists until you need it, and this SCP currently reads just like that.</p>
<p>Beyond that, there's a minor formatting error in the containment procedures; or at least I see it as such: when articles describe the dimensions of an area, the multiplication operator is not capitalized. Minor, yeah, but it sort of throws the style off a bit.</p>
<p>Downvoting for the above reasons, though if improvements are made, I'd be happy to remove that vote or change it to an upvote depending on magnitude of improvements.</p>

							</div>

								
